<div class="card mb-4" id="overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-info-circle me-1"></i>Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI connects to several external systems for configuration deployment, asset sync, and network access control.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-0">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Integration</th><th>What it provides</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>BIND DNS</strong></td><td>Generate and deploy zone files to DNS servers via SSH</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kea DHCP</strong></td><td>Generate and deploy Kea config via SSH; receive lease notifications via API</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ClearPass</strong></td><td>Push managed endpoints to ClearPass; pull RADIUS authentication logs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Snipe-IT</strong></td><td>Pull asset and MAC address data to populate hosts and interfaces</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Aruba CX</strong></td><td>Query switch port status; bounce, reauthenticate, or toggle PoE on ports</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── DNS / BIND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="dns-integration">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-globe me-1"></i>DNS / BIND</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <h6>How it works</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>DashDDI reads all domains, subnets, DNS views, network interfaces, and virtual IPs from the database.</li>
            <li>It generates BIND-compatible zone files (one forward zone per domain, one reverse zone per subnet) and a <code>dashddi.conf</code> file containing all ACL and view definitions.</li>
            <li>The generated files are uploaded to each DNS server via SFTP.</li>
            <li>DashDDI runs <code>rndc reload</code> on the remote server to apply the changes without restarting BIND.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">Deployments are triggered automatically via the async queue when DNS-relevant data changes, or manually from the Servers page or via console command.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Configuring a DNS server in DashDDI</h6>
        <p>Add a server under <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (DNS section). Fields:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Name</td><td>Display label for this server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Hostname</td><td>IP address or hostname DashDDI SSH connects to</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH User</td><td>Linux user account for the SSH connection (default: <code>root</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH Private Key</td><td>Ed25519 key pair generated by DashDDI — copy the public key to the server's <code>authorized_keys</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Remote Zone Path</td><td>Directory on the server where zone files are written (default: <code>/etc/bind/zones</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Key Directory</td><td>Path to the DNSSEC key directory on the remote server (optional)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Server Type</td><td><code>primary</code> or <code>secondary</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Primary Hostname</td><td>For secondary servers: IP/hostname of the primary BIND server (used in <code>primaries</code> directives)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Views</td><td>Which DNS views this server serves</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>SSH key setup</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>Open the DNS server record in DashDDI and click <strong>Generate SSH Key</strong>. DashDDI creates an Ed25519 key pair and displays the public key.</li>
            <li>Copy the public key and append it to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> for the configured SSH user on the BIND server.</li>
            <li>Ensure that user has write access to the remote zone path and permission to run <code>rndc reload</code> (typically via <code>sudoers</code>).</li>
        </ol>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6 id="dns-sudoers">Sudoers configuration</h6>
        <p>DashDDI connects via SSH and runs several commands on the BIND server that require elevated privileges. The SSH user (default: <code>root</code>) needs:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Commands run on the DNS server</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Zone deployment (all servers)</td>
                        <td><code>mkdir</code>, <code>chown</code>, SFTP writes to zone path, <code>rndc reload</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Fetch DS Record</td>
                        <td><code>dig</code> (no sudo), <code>sudo -u bind dnssec-dsfromkey</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>KSK Rollover</td>
                        <td><code>sudo -u bind dnssec-keygen</code>, <code>sudo -u bind dnssec-settime</code>, <code>sudo -u bind dnssec-dsfromkey</code>, <code>sudo rndc loadkeys</code>, <code>sudo rndc sign</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p>Create <code>/etc/sudoers.d/dashddi</code> on each DNS server. Replace <code>dashddi</code> with your configured SSH user:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-2"><code># /etc/sudoers.d/dashddi
#
# Required for rndc commands (zone reload, DNSSEC key loading and signing).
# Note: basic zone deployment runs "rndc reload" without sudo, so this entry
# is only strictly required for DNSSEC features. However, it is also needed
# if the SSH user is non-root and cannot reach the rndc socket directly.
dashddi ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/rndc

# Required for DNSSEC key operations (Fetch DS Record and KSK Rollover).
# dnssec-* tools must run as the bind service user to read/write key files.
dashddi ALL=(bind) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/dnssec-dsfromkey, \
                              /usr/sbin/dnssec-keygen, \
                              /usr/sbin/dnssec-settime</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-info py-2 small mb-3">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>The paths above are for Debian/Ubuntu. Verify the actual paths on your server with
            <code>which rndc dnssec-dsfromkey dnssec-keygen dnssec-settime</code> and adjust the sudoers file accordingly.
            These entries are required even when the SSH user is <code>root</code>, because DashDDI runs commands over a
            non-interactive SSH session which does not have a TTY — sudo will refuse to prompt for a password in that context.
        </div>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>BIND <code>named.conf</code> include</h6>
        <p>DashDDI writes all ACLs, views, and zone stanzas into a single file called <code>dashddi.conf</code> in the configured remote zone path. Add one line to your BIND <code>named.conf</code> to load it:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>include "/etc/bind/zones/dashddi.conf";</code></pre>
        <p class="mb-3">Adjust the path if you changed the Remote Zone Path setting. The generated <code>dashddi.conf</code> includes this line as a comment at the top as a reminder.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Zone file structure</h6>
        <p class="mb-1">Files are written to <code>{remoteZonePath}/{viewName}/</code>:</p>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li><strong>Forward zones</strong> — one file per domain (e.g. <code>example.com.zone</code>)</li>
            <li><strong>Reverse zones</strong> — one file per subnet (e.g. <code>10.0.1.0.zone</code> for IPv4, <code>ip6.arpa</code> zone for IPv6)</li>
            <li><strong><code>dashddi.conf</code></strong> — ACL definitions and all <code>view {}</code> blocks with <code>zone {}</code> stanzas</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-3">Each zone includes SOA records, A/AAAA records from domain records (including interface-linked records), PTR records, and DNSSEC inline-signing directives when a policy is configured. IPv4 reverse zones use RFC 2317 classless delegation where applicable.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Console command</h6>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-1"><code>docker compose exec app bin/console app:dns:generate-config [--output-dir=/tmp/dns-zones] [--deploy]</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Omit <code>--deploy</code> to preview generated files locally without uploading. Results are logged in <strong>Servers → Push Logs</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── DHCP / Kea ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="dhcp-integration">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network me-1"></i>DHCP / Kea</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <h6>How it works</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>DashDDI reads subnets, address blocks, and network interfaces from the database.</li>
            <li>It generates <code>subnets4.json</code> (DHCPv4) and <code>subnets6.json</code> (DHCPv6) in Kea's JSON format.</li>
            <li>The files are uploaded to the Kea server via SFTP.</li>
            <li>DashDDI runs <code>curl</code> through the established SSH session to call the Kea Control Agent on <code>127.0.0.1</code> (localhost only) for a config reload. If the reload fails, the previous config is automatically restored and a second reload is attempted to return Kea to its last working state.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">Deployments are triggered automatically via the async queue when DHCP-relevant data changes, or manually from the Servers page or via console command.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Configuring a DHCP server in DashDDI</h6>
        <p>Add a server under <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (DHCP section). Fields:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Name</td><td>Display label for this server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Hostname</td><td>IP address or hostname DashDDI SSH connects to</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH User</td><td>Linux user account for the SSH connection (default: <code>root</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH Private Key</td><td>Ed25519 key pair generated by DashDDI — copy the public key to the server's <code>authorized_keys</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Remote Path</td><td>Directory on the server where config files are written (default: <code>/etc/kea</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Control Port</td><td>TCP port the Kea Control Agent listens on (default: <code>8000</code>). Required for config reload. DashDDI reaches it via <code>127.0.0.1</code> over the SSH session, so the agent does not need to listen on any external interface.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>DDNS Enabled</td><td>When checked, DashDDI also generates and deploys <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> for Kea's D2 daemon alongside the regular subnet files (see Dynamic DNS below)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>SSH key setup</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>Open the DHCP server record in DashDDI and click <strong>Generate SSH Key</strong>. DashDDI creates an Ed25519 key pair and displays the public key.</li>
            <li>Copy the public key and append it to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> for the configured SSH user on the Kea server.</li>
            <li>Ensure that user has write access to the remote path.</li>
        </ol>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Generated configuration</h6>
        <p class="mb-1"><strong><code>subnets4.json</code> / <code>subnets6.json</code></strong> — for each deployable (non-container) subnet:</p>
        <ul class="mb-2">
            <li><strong>Pools</strong> — all Dynamic address blocks on the subnet</li>
            <li><strong>Router option</strong> (IPv4 only) — the subnet's gateway IP (if set)</li>
            <li><strong>Reservations</strong> — non-deleted interfaces with a static IP and valid MAC, mapping <code>hw-address</code> → <code>ip-address</code> (and optionally <code>hostname</code>)</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-1"><strong><code>global-reservations4.json</code> / <code>global-reservations6.json</code></strong> — flat arrays of hostname-only reservations for dynamic clients:</p>
        <ul class="mb-2">
            <li>Includes every non-deleted interface that has a valid MAC, <strong>no</strong> static IP, and a name entry in any DDNS-enabled domain</li>
            <li>The <code>hostname</code> is the DNS label from that name entry (e.g. <code>mylaptop</code>); Kea appends the subnet's <code>ddns-qualifying-suffix</code> at lease time</li>
            <li>Global reservations match regardless of which subnet the device DHCPs from — useful for devices pre-registered before their location is known</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-1"><strong><code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code></strong> — generated and deployed alongside the subnet files when <strong>DDNS Enabled</strong> is checked. Contains TSIG key blocks, forward DDNS domain entries, and reverse DDNS domain entries for the Kea D2 daemon (see Dynamic DNS below).</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">Interfaces with the all-zeros MAC (<code>00:00:00:00:00:00</code>) are excluded from all reservations.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Console command</h6>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-1"><code>docker compose exec app bin/console app:dhcp:generate-config [--output-dir=/tmp/dhcp] [--reload]</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">Use <code>--output-dir</code> to write files locally for inspection without uploading. Add <code>--reload</code> to trigger a Kea Control Agent reload after deploy. Results are logged in <strong>Servers → Push Logs</strong>.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6 id="kea-ddns">Dynamic DNS (D2 daemon)</h6>
        <p>When <strong>DDNS Enabled</strong> is checked on a DHCP server, DashDDI generates and deploys <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> alongside the regular subnet files. This file configures Kea's D2 daemon to send authenticated DNS UPDATE packets to BIND as leases are granted, keeping forward and reverse DNS in sync with DHCP allocations.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_dns') }}#ddns">DNS guide → Dynamic DNS</a> for the full four-step setup (TSIG key on DNS server → DDNS domain → subnet DDNS domain → DHCP server enabled).</p>

        <p><strong>Install the D2 package on the Kea server</strong></p>
        <p class="mb-3">The D2 daemon ships as a separate package. Install it alongside <code>kea-dhcp4-server</code>:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>apt install kea-dhcp-ddns-server</code></pre>

        <p><strong>Expose D2 to the Kea Control Agent</strong></p>
        <p>Add a <code>d2</code> socket entry to <code>kea-ctrl-agent.conf</code> so DashDDI can signal D2 to reload after deploying <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code>:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>"control-sockets": {
    "d2": {
        "socket-type": "unix",
        "socket-name": "/var/run/kea/kea-ddns-ctrl-socket"
    }
}</code></pre>
        <p class="mb-3">Ensure <code>/var/run/kea/</code> is writable by the kea user. After editing, restart the Control Agent (<code>sudo systemctl restart kea-ctrl-agent</code>) and start the D2 daemon (<code>sudo systemctl enable --now kea-dhcp-ddns</code>).</p>

        <p><strong>What DashDDI generates</strong></p>
        <p class="mb-3">When it deploys DHCP config with DDNS enabled, DashDDI also uploads <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> containing TSIG key blocks (one per configured DNS server), forward DDNS domain entries (one per DDNS-enabled domain), and reverse DDNS domain entries (one per subnet with a DDNS domain assigned). DashDDI resolves DNS server hostnames to IP addresses automatically — D2 requires literal IPs, not hostnames.</p>

        <div class="alert alert-info py-2 small mb-3">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>The TSIG secret appears in plaintext in the deployed <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> and <code>dashddi.conf</code> — this is inherent to the TSIG protocol. Restrict read permissions on both files to the <code>kea</code> and <code>bind</code> service users.
        </div>

        <p><strong>DDNS hostnames for dynamic clients</strong></p>
        <p>When a subnet has a DDNS domain assigned, DashDDI sets <code>ddns-replace-client-name: always</code> in the subnet block, so Kea always uses the hostname from DashDDI's reservation rather than whatever the client reports. Hostname mappings are stored in <code>global-reservations4.json</code> at the Kea global level, so they apply on any subnet — a device pre-registered before its final building is known will still get the right DNS name wherever it connects.</p>
        <p><strong>One-time Kea configuration:</strong> add <code>"reservations-global": true</code> to <code>kea-dhcp4.conf</code> and include the global reservations file:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>"reservations-global": true,
"reservations": &lt;?include "global-reservations4.json"?&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>To give a dynamic device a predictable DNS name:</p>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>Add an interface for the device with its MAC address. Leave the IP address blank.</li>
            <li>On that interface, add a name entry in any DDNS-enabled domain (e.g. name <code>mylaptop</code> in <code>dyn.example.com</code>).</li>
            <li>Redeploy — the device's MAC + label will appear in <code>global-reservations4.json</code>.</li>
            <li>When the device next gets a lease on any DDNS-enabled subnet, Kea registers <code>mylaptop.dyn.example.com</code> in DNS.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Devices with no reservation receive an auto-generated hostname. Devices with a static IP reservation use their primary interface name for DDNS as usual.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6 id="kea-lease-notifications">Configuring Kea to send lease notifications</h6>
        <p>Kea can POST lease events to DashDDI whenever an address is granted or renewed. This requires <strong>Kea 2.2 or later</strong> and uses the <code>run_script</code> hook library.</p>

        <p><strong>Step 1 — Create an API token</strong></p>
        <p class="mb-3">Go to <strong>Other Settings → API Tokens</strong>, create a new token, and grant it permission for the <code>POST /api/dhcp/lease</code> route. Copy the token value — it is shown only once.</p>

        <p><strong>Step 2 — Create the notify script on the Kea server</strong></p>
        <p>Create <code>/usr/share/kea/scripts/notify-dashddi.sh</code> on the Kea server and make it executable (<code>chmod +x</code>):</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>#!/bin/bash
# Called by Kea run_script hook on lease4_select and lease4_renew events
if [[ "$LEASES4_AT0_ADDRESS" == "" ]]; then exit 0; fi

curl -s -X POST https://dashddi.example.com/api/dhcp/lease \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;YOUR_API_TOKEN&gt;" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{
    \"ip-address\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_ADDRESS\",
    \"hw-address\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_HWADDR\",
    \"hostname\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_HOSTNAME\",
    \"valid-lft\": $LEASES4_AT0_VALID_LIFETIME,
    \"expire\": $((LEASES4_AT0_CLTT + LEASES4_AT0_VALID_LIFETIME))
  }"</code></pre>
        <p class="mb-3">Replace <code>dashddi.example.com</code> with your DashDDI hostname and <code>&lt;YOUR_API_TOKEN&gt;</code> with the token from step 1. The script must reside under <code>/usr/share/kea/scripts/</code> — some versions of the <code>run_script</code> hook reject scripts placed elsewhere (e.g. <code>/etc/kea</code>).</p>
        <div class="alert alert-warning py-2 small mb-3">
            <strong>TLS note:</strong> The script runs as the <code>_kea</code> user, which typically does not have access to your organisation's CA bundle. If DashDDI uses a self-signed certificate or an internal CA that is not in the system trust store, <code>curl</code> will fail silently and leases will not be recorded. Add <code>-k</code> to the <code>curl</code> invocation to skip certificate verification, or use <code>--cacert /path/to/ca.crt</code> to trust a specific CA without disabling verification entirely.
        </div>

        <p><strong>Step 3 — Add the hook to <code>kea-dhcp4.conf</code></strong></p>
        <p>Add the <code>run_script</code> hook to the <code>hooks-libraries</code> array in your Kea DHCPv4 configuration:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>"hooks-libraries": [
    {
        "library": "/usr/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_run_script.so",
        "parameters": {
            "name": "/usr/share/kea/scripts/notify-dashddi.sh",
            "sync": false
        }
    }
]</code></pre>
        <p class="mb-3"><code>sync: false</code> lets Kea process leases without waiting for the script to finish, so a slow or unreachable DashDDI instance does not delay DHCP responses.</p>

        <p><strong>Step 4 — Reload Kea</strong></p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>sudo systemctl reload kea-dhcp4</code></pre>

        <p class="mb-0"><strong>DHCPv6</strong> — the same pattern applies for DHCPv6. Use the <code>LEASES6_AT0_*</code> environment variables (<code>LEASES6_AT0_ADDRESS</code>, <code>LEASES6_AT0_HWADDR</code>, etc.) in the script and add the hook to <code>kea-dhcp6.conf</code>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── ClearPass ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="clearpass">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>ClearPass</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI integrates with Aruba ClearPass in two directions: <strong>push</strong> (DashDDI sends host/interface data to ClearPass as managed endpoints) and <strong>pull</strong> (DashDDI retrieves RADIUS authentication session records from ClearPass Insight).</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Configuring a ClearPass server in DashDDI</h6>
        <p>Add a server under <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (ClearPass section). Fields:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Name</td><td>Display label for this connection</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>API URL</td><td>Base URL of the ClearPass REST API (e.g. <code>https://clearpass.example.com</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Client ID</td><td>OAuth application client ID (see below)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Client Secret</td><td>OAuth application client secret</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Verify TLS</td><td>Whether to validate ClearPass's TLS certificate (default: on)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h6>OAuth application setup in ClearPass</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>In ClearPass: <strong>Administration → API Services → API Clients</strong></li>
            <li>Create a new API client with:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Grant Type:</strong> <code>client_credentials</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Operating Mode:</strong> <code>Rest API Client</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Operator Profile:</strong> read/write access to <strong>Endpoint</strong> (for push) and read access to <strong>Session / Insight API</strong> (for pull)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Copy the <strong>Client ID</strong> and <strong>Client Secret</strong> into DashDDI. DashDDI obtains a fresh access token before each API call — no token management is needed on your end.</li>
        </ol>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Push — endpoint management</h6>
        <p>DashDDI pushes network interface data to the ClearPass Endpoint repository so ClearPass policies can reference DashDDI-managed attributes. For each non-deleted interface with a real MAC address, DashDDI creates or updates a ClearPass endpoint with:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>ClearPass attribute</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>mac_address</code></td><td>Interface MAC address</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Always <code>Known</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Managed By</code></td><td>Always <code>DashDDI</code> (used to track managed endpoints)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Device Name</code></td><td>Host name</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Hostname</code></td><td>Primary DNS name of the interface</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IP Address</code></td><td>IPv4 address</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IPv6 Address</code></td><td>IPv6 address</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Subnet</code></td><td>IPv4 CIDR of the subnet</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>VLAN ID</code></td><td>VLAN number from the subnet</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Tags</code></td><td>Pipe-delimited list of host tags</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-1"><strong>Create/Update/Delete behavior:</strong></p>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li><strong>Create</strong> — if the MAC doesn't exist in ClearPass, a new endpoint is created</li>
            <li><strong>Update</strong> — if the MAC exists, endpoint attributes are updated</li>
            <li><strong>Delete</strong> — if an interface is soft-deleted in DashDDI, the corresponding ClearPass endpoint is removed — but only if its <code>Managed By</code> attribute is <code>DashDDI</code>; endpoints created outside DashDDI are never deleted</li>
        </ul>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-1"><code>docker compose exec app bin/console app:push-clearpass [--mac=&lt;mac_address&gt;]</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">Use <code>--mac</code> to push a single interface by MAC address for testing. Pushes can also be scheduled under <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong>.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Pull — authentication logs</h6>
        <p>DashDDI retrieves RADIUS authentication session records from ClearPass Insight and stores them locally, linked to matching host interfaces by MAC address. Each record includes:</p>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li>Session ID, MAC address, auth timestamp, IP address, username</li>
            <li>ClearPass service matched, auth protocol (e.g. EAP-TLS, PEAP)</li>
            <li>NAS IP (switch/AP address), NAS Port ID (switch port), enforcement role, VLAN assigned</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-1"><strong>Pull behavior:</strong> first run fetches sessions from the past hour; subsequent runs fetch only sessions newer than the last pull; duplicates are skipped; sessions are fetched in batches of 1000.</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-1"><code>docker compose exec app bin/console app:pull-clearpass-logs</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Auth log pulls can also be scheduled under <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── Snipe-IT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="snipeit">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-box-seam me-1"></i>Snipe-IT</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI pulls asset data from Snipe-IT and automatically creates or updates host and interface records, keeping your DashDDI data in sync with your asset inventory. The integration is <strong>read-only</strong> — DashDDI never writes to Snipe-IT.</p>

        <h6>How it works</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>DashDDI fetches all active (non-archived) assets from the Snipe-IT API.</li>
            <li>For each asset, it reads MAC addresses from one or more configured custom fields.</li>
            <li>It creates or updates a DashDDI Host and Network Interface for each MAC found.</li>
            <li>Assets that are later archived or deleted in Snipe-IT cause the corresponding DashDDI host to be soft-deleted or unlinked.</li>
        </ol>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Configuring a Snipe-IT server in DashDDI</h6>
        <p>Add a server under <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (Snipe-IT section). Fields:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Name</td><td>Display label for this connection</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>API URL</td><td>Base URL of the Snipe-IT API (e.g. <code>https://snipeit.example.com/api/v1</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>API Key</td><td>Snipe-IT API token — generate one under your user profile in Snipe-IT (<strong>Profile → API Keys → Create</strong>). Read-only access is sufficient.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>MAC Custom Fields</td><td>Comma-separated Snipe-IT custom field names that contain MAC addresses. Append <code>:alias</code> to control the interface name in DashDDI (e.g. <code>MAC Address, WiFi MAC Address:wifi, Management MAC:mgmt</code>). Without an alias, a short name is derived automatically. Multiple MAC values in a single field can be separated by newlines, semicolons, or commas.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Verify TLS</td><td>Whether to validate Snipe-IT's TLS certificate. Disable if using a self-signed cert.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="alert alert-info py-2 small mb-3">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>Snipe-IT has no built-in MAC address field — you must create custom fields in Snipe-IT and add them to your asset fieldsets before they can be mapped here.
        </div>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Category → subnet mapping</h6>
        <p class="mb-3">Configure mappings between Snipe-IT asset categories and DashDDI subnets on the Snipe-IT server record under <strong>Category Subnet Maps</strong>. When a new interface is created from a Snipe-IT asset, DashDDI automatically assigns it to the subnet configured for that asset's category.</p>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Sync behavior</h6>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What DashDDI does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Asset MAC not known to DashDDI</td><td>Creates a new Host and NetworkInterface. Tags the host with <code>snipeit</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Asset matches an existing unlinked host</td><td>Adopts the host (links it to the asset, preserves the existing name).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Multiple existing hosts each match one of the asset's MACs</td><td>Merges them into a single host linked to the asset.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Subsequent sync runs</td><td>Updates host name to match asset name (unless manually changed since last sync); soft-deletes interfaces whose MACs no longer appear; restores interfaces whose MACs reappear; backfills interface names that are still unset.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Asset archived/deleted — adopted host</td><td>Removes the <code>snipeit</code> tag and unlinks the asset; host is preserved.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Asset archived/deleted — sync-created host</td><td>Soft-deletes the host and its interfaces.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-1"><code>docker compose exec app bin/console app:pull-snipe-it [--server-id=&lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Omit <code>--server-id</code> to sync all configured servers. The command prints a summary of created/updated/deleted/skipped counts. Syncs can also be scheduled under <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── Aruba CX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="aruba">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-router me-1"></i>Aruba CX Switches</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI can query Aruba CX switches to retrieve port access information and perform port management actions. This integration works in conjunction with ClearPass authentication logs — the NAS IP and NAS Port ID from an auth log entry are used to identify and act on the switch port a device is connected to.</p>

        <h6>Capabilities</h6>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li><strong>View port client info</strong> — connected device, MAC address, VLAN, authentication status, and enforcement role</li>
            <li><strong>Reauthenticate</strong> — trigger 802.1X reauthentication without disrupting connectivity</li>
            <li><strong>Bounce port</strong> — administratively shut down and re-enable the port, forcing the device to reconnect</li>
            <li><strong>PoE bounce</strong> — power-cycle the PoE output (useful for IP phones and access points)</li>
        </ul>

        <hr class="my-3">
        <h6>Configuring a switch in DashDDI</h6>
        <p>Add a switch under <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (Aruba section). Fields:</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Name</td><td>Display label for this switch</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Username</td><td>Username for REST API and SSH login</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Password</td><td>Password for REST API and SSH password auth</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH Private Key</td><td>SSH private key for key-based authentication (alternative to password for SSH)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>REST API Version</td><td>API version for REST calls (default: <code>v10.12</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Verify TLS</td><td>Whether to validate the switch's TLS certificate. Default: off — most switches use self-signed certs.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-3">The switch's IP address is not stored on the server record — it is taken at action time from the NAS IP recorded in the ClearPass auth log.</p>

        <h6>Connection methods</h6>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Used for</th><th>Requirements</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>REST API</td><td>Port info retrieval (primary); bounce port (primary)</td><td>Username + Password</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SSH</td><td>Port info retrieval (fallback); bounce port (fallback); reauthenticate; PoE bounce</td><td>Username + Password or SSH Private Key</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-3">DashDDI tries REST first for port info queries and for bounce port, falling back to SSH if the REST call fails. Reauthenticate and PoE bounce always use SSH.</p>

        <h6>Usage flow</h6>
        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>A device authenticates through ClearPass. DashDDI pulls the auth log, which records the NAS IP (switch) and NAS Port ID (port).</li>
            <li>From the auth log entry in DashDDI, click the port info button to view the connected device, VLAN, auth method, and role.</li>
            <li>From the same view, trigger reauthentication, bounce, or PoE bounce on that port.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Port IDs in ClearPass logs are typically in <code>NAS-Port-ID</code> format (e.g. <code>1/1/5 - Profile Name</code>). DashDDI normalizes these automatically, extracting just the port identifier before sending it to the switch.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# ── REST API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="api">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-code-slash me-1"></i>REST API</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI exposes a REST API covering all major resources — hosts, interfaces, subnets, domains, records, virtual IPs, and more. Interactive API documentation with request/response schemas and the ability to test calls is available at <a href="{{ path('api_docs') }}"><strong>API Docs</strong></a>.</p>
        <p>Authentication uses Bearer tokens (<code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>). Create a <strong>general token</strong> under <strong>Other Settings → API Tokens</strong> and grant only the per-route permissions that integration needs. For per-host automation, use <strong>host-scoped tokens</strong> instead — see below.</p>

        <h6 id="api-host-token-management" class="mt-3">Host token management (admin API)</h6>
        <p>These endpoints are available to any general token with the appropriate route permission.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>POST</code></td><td><code>/api/hosts/{id}/token</code></td><td>Generate (or regenerate) a host-scoped token. Returns the raw token value once — store it immediately.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DELETE</code></td><td><code>/api/hosts/{id}/token</code></td><td>Revoke the host's token. Returns 204 on success, 404 if no token exists.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small"><strong>POST</strong> response: <code>{"token": "&lt;raw&gt;", "id": &lt;token_id&gt;, "host_id": &lt;host_id&gt;}</code></p>

        <h6 id="api-self" class="mt-3">Host self-service endpoints (<code>/api/self</code>)</h6>
        <p>These endpoints are only accessible with a <strong>host-scoped token</strong>. Requests are accepted only from IP addresses assigned to the token's host's network interfaces. They are designed to support ACME DNS-01 certificate automation on each host without granting broad DNS write access.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/api/self/host</code></td><td>Return the host's name, its interfaces (id, MAC, IPv4, IPv6), and all DNS records attached to each interface. Only records in domains with at least one <a href="{{ path('user_guide_dns') }}#views">public view</a> are included; domains with no views assigned are treated as globally accessible.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>POST</code></td><td><code>/api/self/dns-challenge</code></td><td>Create a <code>_acme-challenge.*</code> TXT record for ACME DNS-01 validation.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DELETE</code></td><td><code>/api/self/dns-challenge</code></td><td>Delete the matching <code>_acme-challenge.*</code> TXT record after validation is complete.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p><strong><code>POST /api/self/dns-challenge</code></strong> request body:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-2"><code>{
  "fqdn": "web01.example.com",
  "validation": "&lt;acme-token-value&gt;"
}</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small"><code>fqdn</code> must match one of the DNS records already assigned to the host's interfaces. For an apex record (<code>@</code>), pass the bare domain name (e.g. <code>example.com</code>). The challenge record is created as <code>_acme-challenge.web01.example.com</code> (or <code>_acme-challenge.example.com</code> for the apex) and linked to the same interface as the source record. The <strong>DELETE</strong> request uses the same body to identify and remove the record.</p>

        <h6 id="api-certbot-plugin" class="mt-3">Certbot plugin (<code>certbot-dns-dashddi</code>)</h6>
        <p>The recommended approach for certificate automation is the <strong>certbot-dns-dashddi</strong> plugin, which handles challenge creation and cleanup automatically and supports a <code>dashddi-certbot</code> wrapper that discovers the host's FQDNs from DashDDI so you don't have to list them manually.</p>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Installation</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Certbot and the plugin must share the same Python environment. On Debian/Ubuntu:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>apt install -y python3-venv
python3 -m venv /opt/certbot
/opt/certbot/bin/pip install certbot
/opt/certbot/bin/pip install git+https://github.com/goshencollege/dashddi/#subdirectory=certbot-dns-dashddi
ln -s /opt/certbot/bin/certbot /usr/local/bin/certbot
ln -s /opt/certbot/bin/dashddi-certbot /usr/local/bin/dashddi-certbot</code></pre>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Credentials file</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Create <code>/etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini</code> (chmod 600):</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>dns_dashddi_url = https://dashddi.example.com
dns_dashddi_token = &lt;host-scoped-token&gt;

# Optional — only needed if DashDDI uses a self-signed or internal CA cert:
#   dns_dashddi_ca_cert = false                       # disable SSL verification (dev only)
#   dns_dashddi_ca_cert = /etc/ssl/certs/internal.pem # custom CA bundle</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small">Generate the token from the host detail page in DashDDI: <strong>Hosts → &lt;hostname&gt; → Host API Token → Generate Token</strong>. The token is accepted only from this host's IP addresses.</p>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Automatic FQDN discovery</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">The <code>dashddi-certbot</code> wrapper queries <code>/api/self/host</code> to find all A/AAAA FQDNs for this host and requests a single SAN certificate covering them all:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>dashddi-certbot --credentials /etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini</code></pre>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Manual domain list</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">To specify domains explicitly, use certbot directly:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>certbot certonly \
  --authenticator dns-dashddi \
  --dns-dashddi-credentials /etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini \
  -d web01.example.com -d app.example.com</code></pre>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Automatic renewal (systemd timer)</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Create <code>/etc/systemd/system/dashddi-certbot.service</code> and <code>dashddi-certbot.timer</code>:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>[Unit]
Description=Renew certificates via DashDDI

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/dashddi-certbot --credentials /etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini</code></pre>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>[Unit]
Description=Run dashddi-certbot twice daily

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 03,15:00:00
RandomizedDelaySec=1h
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target</code></pre>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now dashddi-certbot.timer</code></pre>

        <h6 id="api-ansible" class="mt-4">Ansible role (<code>dashddi_certbot</code>)</h6>
        <p>An Ansible role is included in the repository at <code>certbot-dns-dashddi/ansible/roles/dashddi_certbot/</code>. It automates the entire setup: generates a host-scoped token via the DashDDI API, installs certbot and the plugin into a virtualenv, writes the credentials file, and deploys a systemd renewal timer.</p>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Required variables</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
        <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
            <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_url</code></td><td>Base URL of your DashDDI instance</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_admin_token</code></td><td>General-purpose token with <code>api_hosts_index</code> and <code>api_hosts_token_generate</code> route permissions</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Optional variables (with defaults)</p>
        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
        <table class="table table-sm table-bordered small mb-0">
            <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_host_name</code></td><td><code>inventory_hostname</code></td><td>Host name in DashDDI (must match exactly)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_credentials_path</code></td><td><code>/etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini</code></td><td>Where to write the credentials file</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_venv_path</code></td><td><code>/opt/certbot</code></td><td>Virtualenv for certbot and the plugin</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_propagation_seconds</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td>DNS propagation wait passed to certbot</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dashddi_force_token_regenerate</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Set to <code>true</code> to revoke and replace the existing token</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>

        <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">Usage</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Run the included example playbook against your managed hosts. Set credentials in <code>group_vars</code> or pass via vault:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-2"><code>ansible-playbook -i inventory certbot-dns-dashddi/ansible/certbot.yml \
  -e dashddi_url=https://dashddi.example.com \
  -e @vault.yml</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">To force token regeneration on a specific host:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>ansible-playbook -i inventory certbot-dns-dashddi/ansible/certbot.yml \
  -e dashddi_force_token_regenerate=true \
  --limit web01.example.com</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small">The role is idempotent: if the credentials file already exists and <code>dashddi_force_token_regenerate</code> is false, token generation is skipped. Installation and systemd unit deployment always run.</p>

        <h6 id="api-acme" class="mt-4">Manual hook approach (curl)</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small">If installing Python packages is not practical, you can drive the same endpoints manually using Certbot's <code>--manual</code> authenticator:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-2"><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/dashddi-auth.sh
# Set DASHDDI_TOKEN to the host-scoped token value.

DASHDDI_URL="https://dashddi.example.com"
TOKEN="${DASHDDI_TOKEN:?DASHDDI_TOKEN must be set}"

curl -fsSL -X POST "${DASHDDI_URL}/api/self/dns-challenge" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{\"fqdn\": \"${CERTBOT_DOMAIN}\", \"validation\": \"${CERTBOT_VALIDATION}\"}"

# Wait for DNS propagation, then signal Certbot to continue
sleep 30</code></pre>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/clean/dashddi-cleanup.sh

DASHDDI_URL="https://dashddi.example.com"
TOKEN="${DASHDDI_TOKEN:?DASHDDI_TOKEN must be set}"

curl -fsSL -X DELETE "${DASHDDI_URL}/api/self/dns-challenge" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{\"fqdn\": \"${CERTBOT_DOMAIN}\", \"validation\": \"${CERTBOT_VALIDATION}\"}"</code></pre>

        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Full endpoint reference — including request parameters, field descriptions, and response examples — is in the <a href="{{ path('api_docs') }}">API Docs</a>.</p>

        <h5 id="proxmox-acme" class="mt-4">Proxmox VE ACME certificates</h5>
        <p>Proxmox VE can use DashDDI to perform DNS-01 challenges for its built-in ACME certificate management. The plugin uses the same host-scoped token and <code>/api/self/dns-challenge</code> endpoints as the certbot plugin.</p>

        <h6 id="proxmox-acme-install" class="mt-3">Installation</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Run <code>install.sh</code> as root on each Proxmox node. It copies the plugin script, merges the schema entry (with a backup), validates the JSON, and restarts the necessary services:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>sudo bash proxmox-acme-dashddi/install.sh</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">To skip the automatic restart (e.g. during a maintenance window):</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>sudo bash proxmox-acme-dashddi/install.sh --no-restart
systemctl restart pveproxy pvedaemon</code></pre>
        <p class="text-muted small">The script is idempotent — re-running it updates the plugin in place. The schema entry being merged is:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small overflow-auto mb-3"><code>"dashddi": {
  "fields": {
    "DASHDDI_API_URL": {
      "description": "DashDDI base URL (e.g. https://dashddi.example.com)",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "DASHDDI_API_TOKEN": {
      "description": "Host-scoped API token (from the host detail page in DashDDI)",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "DASHDDI_CA_CERT": {
      "description": "Optional: PEM CA bundle path, or 'false' to skip SSL verification",
      "type": "string",
      "optional": 1
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

        <h6 id="proxmox-acme-configure" class="mt-3">Configuration</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-1">Generate a host-scoped token from the host detail page in DashDDI: <strong>Hosts → &lt;node name&gt; → Host API Token → Generate Token</strong>. Then in Proxmox:</p>
        <ol class="text-muted small mb-3">
            <li>Go to <strong>Datacenter → ACME → Challenge Plugins → Add</strong></li>
            <li>Set Plugin Type to <strong>DNS</strong> and select <strong>dashddi</strong> from the dropdown</li>
            <li>Enter <code>DASHDDI_API_URL</code> and <code>DASHDDI_API_TOKEN</code></li>
            <li>If DashDDI uses a self-signed or internal CA cert, set <code>DASHDDI_CA_CERT</code> to the PEM bundle path, or <code>false</code> to skip verification</li>
            <li>Go to <strong>Node → System → Certificates → ACME</strong>, add a domain entry using the <strong>dashddi</strong> plugin, and click <strong>Order Certificates Now</strong></li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-muted small mb-0"><strong>Note:</strong> ACME plugin <em>configuration</em> is stored in <code>/etc/pve/priv/acme/plugins.cfg</code> and cluster-replicated automatically. The script file and schema edit are local to each node and must be reapplied if <code>libproxmox-acme-plugins</code> is ever purged, or when provisioning a new node.</p>
    </div>
</div>

